WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.413
High-quality detections help analysts focus their attention.

2
00:00:03.413 --> 00:00:11.983
Poorly tuned rules may generate excessive false positives, consume investigation time, and erode confidence in the detection program.

3
00:00:11.983 --> 00:00:22.949
Detection authors should consider expected activity, environmental context, baseline patterns, known administrative workflows, and the operational burden created by alerts.

4
00:00:22.949 --> 00:00:27.199
A rule that fires constantly may hide the activity it was meant to reveal.

5
00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:30.562
Good detections balance sensitivity and specificity.

6
00:00:30.562 --> 00:00:33.416
Sensitivity helps catch more relevant activity.

7
00:00:33.416 --> 00:00:36.329
Specificity helps reduce irrelevant matches.

8
00:00:36.329 --> 00:00:43.164
The right balance depends on the risk, the available telemetry, and what analysts are expected to do with the signal.

9
00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:46.148
Noise reduction should not mean hiding uncertainty.

10
00:00:46.148 --> 00:00:49.969
Some detections are intentionally broad because the behavior matters.

11
00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:58.839
In those cases, the rule should include context, enrichment, severity guidance, and triage notes so analysts can make faster decisions.
